WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.780
Hey, everyone, welcome to Models and Agents episode 27.

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:04.540
It's April 7th, 2026.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.540
You're daily briefing on the AI models and agents

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:08.780
that are changing everything.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:11.720
And no, not those kinds of models and agents.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:12.780
Let's get into it.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.400
Gemma 4 delivers massive gains across European languages

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.340
while a 25.6 million rust model achieves

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.320
50 times faster inference via hybrid attention.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.500
This week, the AI world is showing us

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.420
three different ways to get better results.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.200
Pure scaling is still powerful, but clever architecture

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.700
can deliver ridiculous speedups at the edge.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.200
And agentic workflows are collapsing

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.740
entire traditional pipelines into simple tool using loops.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.560
There is real measurable progress on all three fronts,

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.020
which makes this an especially fun week to dig in.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.860
The top story comes from an independent researcher

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:53.120
who built a brand new 25.6 million parameter language model

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.040
trained entirely on rust code.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:57.360
What makes it interesting is not just the size,

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:58.740
but how it was built from scratch

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.740
to test a very specific idea.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.300
The author forked PyTorch and Triton

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.520
to implement a hybrid attention mechanism.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.320
It combines local window detention

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.540
with a GRU-like recurrent state mixed together

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:11.460
by a learned gate.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:13.920
They also replaced standard attention

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.000
with a linear quadratic linear pattern.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.060
The results on inference speed are dramatic.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:24.440
On an RTX 4060T, the model jumped from 5.6 tokens

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.280
per second to 286 tokens per second.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.800
That is a 50 times speedup.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.620
It achieves this by keeping only a recent window

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.900
in the KV cache while the recurrent state

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.180
lives in tiny, fixed size registers.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.200
Expanding the training data from 31 megabytes

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.420
to 173 megabytes of rust crates delivered

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.700
much larger reductions in validation loss

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.320
than the architectural tricks alone.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.160
Final perplexity landed at 2.15.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.540
The model generates plausible syntax,

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.900
but still shows weak semantic consistency

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:55.180
and some repetition.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.280
The clearest takeaway is that at these tiny scales,

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.960
high-quality data usually beats clever architecture.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:03.140
Yet the hybrid mechanism itself

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:05.020
is a compelling inference optimization

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:07.380
worth testing on your own edge workloads.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:09.520
Now let's talk about Gemma 4.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:13.100
Google's latest model, especially the 31 billion parameter

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.540
version, has climbed into the top three or better

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.260
on nearly every major European language leaderboard

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:19.360
according to Euro-Eval.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.040
It ranks third on Dutch, second on Danish,

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.840
third on English, first on Finnish, second on French,

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.000
fifth on German, second on Italian, and third on Swedish.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.720
These are huge gains for a model of its size,

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:32.400
often beating much larger models.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:35.960
Community members are now wondering whether real-world usage

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:38.000
matches the impressive benchmark numbers,

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.460
especially for non-English coding, legal,

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:41.400
or creative tasks.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.060
Early feedback suggests the multilingual improvements

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:46.080
are noticeable even in the smaller variants.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.900
That makes Gemma 4 an attractive drop-in replacement

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:51.520
for Claude or GPT-40 class models

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:54.060
when token cost or latency matters to you.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:57.200
One developer is already prototyping a clever setup

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:00.940
using Gemma 4 as a local preprocessor for Claude code.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.500
They built a bun-based proxy that sits

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.040
in front of the Claude API.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:10.160
Gemma 4E2B running through lama.cpp translates

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:13.020
Korean to English, prunes irrelevant context,

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.340
and optionally performs initial reasoning

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:16.660
before the expensive call.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:20.300
The proxy caches results in SQLite using WAL mode.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.060
Intel Mac users are particularly interested

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:26.560
in real tokens per second numbers for lama.cpp

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:28.260
on non-Apple Silicon hardware.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.620
The open question is whether pre-supplying

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:33.780
that reasoning actually reduces the paid model's token usage

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.220
or whether Claude simply re-does the work internally.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:38.960
On the agent side, there is growing excitement

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.880
around using coding agents as general purpose agents.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.300
One Reddit user shared how they took a pie coding agent SDK

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.080
with red, right, and bash tools

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.460
and used it to handle document knowledge bases.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.480
They performed structured extraction

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:55.160
from over 100 PDFs and ran database benchmarking

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:57.000
all without any vector database.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.760
Traditional pipelines collapse into agent loops.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.700
Retrieval augmented generation becomes red index,

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:04.400
choose files, open them.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.300
ETL becomes write script, run it, inspect output,

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:09.860
retry if needed.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:13.520
The pattern has already scaled to roughly 600 documents.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.280
The main uncertainties are what breaks first

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:19.460
at larger scale, cost, latency, reliability,

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:20.480
or context management.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:22.840
The author open sourced the code

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:24.640
so others can inspect and build on it.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.440
Another fascinating project is VisionClaw.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.140
It turns Metareban smart glasses

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:32.420
into an always on egocentric agent.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.140
The system continuously perceives the world

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.820
and lets users initiate speech-driven tasks.

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:40.180
You can add objects to an Amazon cart,

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:43.180
generate notes from documents, create calendar events

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:46.120
from posters or control IoT devices.

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:49.040
A controlled lab study with 12 participants

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.440
in a longitudinal deployment with five users

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.060
showed faster task completion

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:54.960
and reduced interaction overhead.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:57.760
The comparison was against both non-agentic

00:04:57.760 --> 00:04:59.280
and non-always on baselines.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:03.040
Interaction patterns shifted toward opportunistic initiation

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:05.460
and delegation rather than manual control.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.640
We are also seeing more specialized agent work,

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:11.120
a hackathon project created VUHA,

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:16.180
an AI site reliability engineering agent built on GLM 5.1.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:18.980
It functions as a triple cloud recovery orchestrator

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:21.780
covering AWS, Azure, and GCP.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:25.220
The agent uses a fast API control plane

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:27.540
and an evolutionary SU light memory

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:30.120
that learns from every human approved failover.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:33.900
It gathers context, proposes fixes in JSON format,

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:36.120
and only acts after human approval.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:38.780
A reflection phase writes incident learnings

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:41.220
back into memory so future diagnoses improve.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:44.620
There is even a live demo where you can hard kill nodes

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:46.980
and watch the system react in real time.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:49.220
On the practical and community side,

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:52.020
there is new guidance about using minimal container images

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:54.340
to fortify AI agent security.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:56.840
Drastically shrinking container images

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:59.420
reduces the attack surface for deployed agents.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:03.420
You get fewer dependencies, smaller software bill of materials

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:04.900
and faster cold starts.

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:07.520
These are practical wins for both security

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:10.480
and operational efficiency when running autonomous agents

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:11.060
at scale.

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:13.600
On a more serious note, security researchers

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:17.040
identified critical remote code execution vulnerabilities

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.300
in publicly deployed flow eyes instances.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:22.980
The issue carries a CVSS score of 10.0

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.520
and is under active exploitation in the wild.

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:29.160
More than 12,000 instances are currently exposed.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:33.120
Teams running flow eyes or similar low code agent builders

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:36.540
should immediately isolate internet facing instances,

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:40.380
apply available patches and follow responsible disclosure

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:40.940
practices.

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:44.600
This incident underscores the operational security

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:48.020
debt that comes with rapidly shipping agent tooling.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:52.060
OK, let's pop the hood on this hybrid attention approach

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:54.760
because it is more subtle than most people realize.

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:57.120
Everyone talks about hybrid attention

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.980
as if it is a simple performance dial you turn on.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.780
In practice, it is a carefully engineered compromise

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:06.920
between local syntax, long range state, and memory bandwidth.

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:10.620
The core insight is to split attention into three stages,

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.980
a linear projection, a narrow quadratic window

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:16.360
that still captures short-range dependencies

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:18.360
and another linear stage.

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:22.160
At the same time, they inject a GRU-style recurrent state

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:23.340
that compresses history.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:26.700
A learned gate then blends the local and recurrent path

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:29.420
so the model can decide token by token

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:32.300
whether syntax or distant context matters more.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:35.720
By keeping only the recent window in the KV cache

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:38.920
while the recurrent state lives in tiny fixed size registers,

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:41.180
they get the 50 times speed up.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:44.360
The quadratic middle layer never sees the full context.

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:46.680
The quality cost trade-off is revealing.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:51.180
Perplexity barely moves yet semantic coherence still lags

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:53.400
because the recurrent compression is lossy.

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:55.920
Above roughly 30 million parameters,

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:58.060
the architectural gains shrinks rapidly

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:00.180
and data scale reasserts dominance.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:03.380
The gacha that bites most teams is assuming

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:06.200
the hybrid mechanism will magically fix repetition.

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:08.360
It reduces latency dramatically

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:11.080
but does not replace the need for high quality,

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:13.920
diverse training data, and proper temperature scheduling.

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:16.060
So when should you actually reach for this

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:17.260
versus the alternative?

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:20.040
Use hybrid attention when you are latency bound

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:22.860
on edge hardware and your context is mostly local

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:24.900
such as code, chat, or control loops.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:27.700
For deep reasoning or long document synthesis,

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:29.940
classic transformer with efficient attention

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:33.880
like flash attention to or MLA remains the safer default.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:37.500
If you have not tried the open sourced coding agent SDK

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:41.680
from the local LLAMA post, this week is a great time.

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:46.340
Replace one manual ETL or retrieval augmented generation pipeline

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:48.040
with a red-right bash agent loop

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:50.860
and see how far you get before context or cost becomes

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:51.680
the bottleneck.

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:55.460
Download Gemma 4, either the 9 billion or 31 billion version

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:56.600
via hugging face.

00:08:57.080 --> 00:08:59.140
Run it on the European languages you actually use

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:01.320
in Compare Korean or French token efficiency

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:05.760
versus Claude 3.5 or 4 to quantify potential API savings.

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:10.140
Test the hybrid attention 25.6 million parameter rust model

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:13.080
or replicate the linear quadratic linear pattern

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:18.240
in your own small decoder on a local 406 OTI or laptop GPU.

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:21.640
Measure real-world tokens per second

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:24.440
versus a standard transformer of similar size.

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:28.280
Deploy a vision-cloth style egocentric prompting setup

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:31.840
on your own Rayban glasses or any always on camera feed.

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:34.640
Try opportunistic commands like add this to cart

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:36.720
or summarize this poster and notice

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:38.960
how delegation changes your interaction habits.

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:42.040
And if you run flow-ice or any low-code agent builder,

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:44.400
audit your internet-exposed instances today

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:46.820
and consider moving to minimal container images

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:48.240
or air-gapped setups.

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:52.240
Security debt compounds fast once agents can call tools.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:55.500
On the horizon, keep an eye on continued community exploration

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:59.740
of memory topologies in lifelong LLM multi-agent systems.

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:03.020
More agentic federated learning papers and prototypes

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:05.920
are likely following the agentic FL paradigm shift.

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:08.960
A 2A agentization benchmarks and tooling

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:11.860
should see rapid iteration now that the formal framework

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:14.640
is public and watch for production deployments

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:16.300
of cognitive fabric nodes middleware,

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:18.800
which are already showing more than 10% gains

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:20.800
on hotpot QA and Musikui.

